      "problem": "The displacement $s$ of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=12{t}^{4}-3{t}^{3}+t.$ Find the particle's acceleration at time $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12{t}^{4}-3{t}^{3}+t) \\\\  & =48{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(48{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =144{t}^{2}-18t\\end{aligned}\n$$",
